    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d768">

                <pb facs="0812" n="802" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg802"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.5151/7–2649: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Douglas</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-07-26T18:00:00+01:00">July 26, 1949—6 p.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 6:45 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2954. Deptel 2536, July 21, re freezing Chinese foreign exchange balances.
                        Dening,<anchor n="46" xml:id="d768-f1"/> Foreign Office, re paragraph 2 of
                    Deptel under reference, states Foreign Office has not been informed opinion Hong
                    Kong [and] Shanghai bank manager and does not contemplate freezing <pb
                        facs="0813" n="803" xml:id="pg803"/> Chinese foreign exchange assets at
                    present time. Believes Shanghai British unduly panicky although easy to say this
                    when 10,000 miles away. Will consult Treasury and inform Embassy.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Douglas</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d768-f1" n="46" xml:id="d768-f1-content">
                <p>Maberly E. Dening, British Assistant Under-Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs
                    (Far East).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
